{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TDFUkTCfzX7kzHSJWvzo9P","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Dobry Whitman Roesset 1971 shear strains modal superposition soil layer seismic earthquake engineering\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Soil amplification: some refinements - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/026172778490041X\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic History Analysis of Asymmetric Buildings with Soil ...\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9445(2009)135:2(101)\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear Analysis of Building Structures Resting on Soft Soil Considering Soil–Structure Interaction and Structure–Soil–Structure Interaction | Journal of The Institution of Engineers (India): Series A | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40030-024-00788-3\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Soil-Structure Interaction in Buildings. I: Analytical Methods | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 125, No 1\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/(ASCE)1090-0241(1999)125:1(26)\"},{\"title\":\"Soil Structure Interaction The Early Stages - Journal of Applied Science and Engineering\",\"url\":\"http://jase.tku.edu.tw/articles/jase-201303-16-1-01\"},{\"title\":\"Methodologies for Seismic Soil–Structure Interaction Analysis in the\",\"url\":\"https://www-pub.iaea.org/MTCD/publications/PDF/TE-1990web.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Shear-Wave Velocity–Based Probabilistic and Deterministic Assessment of Seismic Soil Liquefaction Potential | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 139, No 3\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.com/doi/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000743\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Seismic response of deep soft soil sites with varying shear wave velocities\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2024.1488519/full\"},{\"title\":\"Modal analysis and superposition for dynamic response of structures with discontinuities using HybriDFEM - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0168874X25000496\"},{\"title\":\"Effect of soil on structural response to wind and earthquake - Novak - 1974 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/eqe.4290030107\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the work related to your query. \n\nThe fundamentals of amplification due to vertically propagating SH waves in soil deposits have been formulated by Roesset & Whitman and Dobry et al. More specifically, Roesset, J. M., Whitman, R. V., and Dobry, R. published \"Modal analysis for structures with foundation interaction\" in the Journal of Structural Division in 1973.\n\nThis work appears to be foundational in the field of seismic earthquake engineering. For multi-layered soil strata, the transfer matrix approach of Thomson and Haskell is usually applied, which relates to the methods discussed in the Roesset, Whitman, and Dobry publication on modal analysis.\n\nThe search results indicate that the fundamentals formulated by Roesset & Whitman and Dobry et al. were further refined by Dobry et al., Roesset, and Kausel & Roesset, showing the evolution of these analytical approaches in seismic soil engineering.\n\nIt should be noted that the publication date from the search results shows 1973 rather than 1971, though the work was clearly influential in developing modal superposition methods for analyzing seismic response in layered soil systems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed3db8e9-bddf-493a-90e5-1f46bbf22517","uuid":"f1b547de-03b2-43f2-8f9e-a9d1e5c0a97b","timestamp":"2026-03-29T13:48:12.536Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Dobry Whitman Roesset 1971 shear strains modal superposition soil layer seismic earthquake engineering","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Jnv21CDcKe8ky2g8PByXm2","content":[{"title":"Soil amplification: some refinements - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/026172778490041X"},{"title":"Seismic History Analysis of Asymmetric Buildings with Soil ...","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9445(2009)135:2(101)"},{"title":"Nonlinear Analysis of Building Structures Resting on Soft Soil Considering Soil–Structure Interaction and Structure–Soil–Structure Interaction | Journal of The Institution of Engineers (India): Series A | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40030-024-00788-3"},{"title":"Seismic Soil-Structure Interaction in Buildings. I: Analytical Methods | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 125, No 1","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/(ASCE)1090-0241(1999)125:1(26)"},{"title":"Soil Structure Interaction The Early Stages - Journal of Applied Science and Engineering","url":"http://jase.tku.edu.tw/articles/jase-201303-16-1-01"},{"title":"Methodologies for Seismic Soil–Structure Interaction Analysis in the","url":"https://www-pub.iaea.org/MTCD/publications/PDF/TE-1990web.pdf"},{"title":"Shear-Wave Velocity–Based Probabilistic and Deterministic Assessment of Seismic Soil Liquefaction Potential | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 139, No 3","url":"https://ascelibrary.com/doi/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000743"},{"title":"Frontiers | Seismic response of deep soft soil sites with varying shear wave velocities","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2024.1488519/full"},{"title":"Modal analysis and superposition for dynamic response of structures with discontinuities using HybriDFEM - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0168874X25000496"},{"title":"Effect of soil on structural response to wind and earthquake - Novak - 1974 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/eqe.4290030107"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about the work related to your query. \n\nThe fundamentals of amplification due to vertically propagating SH waves in soil deposits have been formulated by Roesset & Whitman and Dobry et al. More specifically, Roesset, J. M., Whitman, R. V., and Dobry, R. published \"Modal analysis for structures with foundation interaction\" in the Journal of Structural Division in 1973.\n\nThis work appears to be foundational in the field of seismic earthquake engineering. For multi-layered soil strata, the transfer matrix approach of Thomson and Haskell is usually applied, which relates to the methods discussed in the Roesset, Whitman, and Dobry publication on modal analysis.\n\nThe search results indicate that the fundamentals formulated by Roesset & Whitman and Dobry et al. were further refined by Dobry et al., Roesset, and Kausel & Roesset, showing the evolution of these analytical approaches in seismic soil engineering.\n\nIt should be noted that the publication date from the search results shows 1973 rather than 1971, though the work was clearly influential in developing modal superposition methods for analyzing seismic response in layered soil systems."],"durationSeconds":7.313706499999971}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VEJQ2sDUkCUTyec5ZKqRxS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"1D shear wave propagation uniform soil layer free surface rock base transfer function acceleration closed form equation derivation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0267726122003773\"},{\"title\":\"Theory and Examples — TransferFunctionTool 1.0.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://nheri-simcenter.github.io/TFT-Documentation/theory.html\"},{\"title\":\"One dimensional (1D) shear wave propagation through a soil layer | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/One-dimensional-1D-shear-wave-propagation-through-a-soil-layer_fig24_308942249\"},{\"title\":\"Analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils\",\"url\":\"https://idus.us.es/bitstreams/f5bf7b6a-8d1f-466a-99fb-6f80bc91b63c/download\"},{\"title\":\"Dispersive SH-surface waves in soil deposits of variable shear modulus - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726105800041\"},{\"title\":\"Simulation of the propagation of a cylindrical shear wave\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.04750\"},{\"title\":\"17th World Conference on Earthquake Engineering, 17WCEE\",\"url\":\"https://wcee.nicee.org/wcee/article/17WCEE/4f-0001.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"PACIFIC EARTHQUAKE ENGINEERING RESEARCH CENTER Technical Manual for Strata\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Example 2: Multi-Material Site Response Analysis with Transfer Function Comparison — Femora (Fast Efficient Meshing for OpenSees-based Resilience Analysis) 0.5.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://femora.io/introduction/SiteResponseExamples/example2.html\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis of Nonlinear Soil-Structure Interaction Effects on ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1601.02352\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found comprehensive information about 1D shear wave propagation transfer functions for uniform soil layers with free surfaces and rock bases. Here's what the research literature covers:\n\n## Key Theoretical Framework\n\nThe method considers balance of linear momentum (equilibrium) and the constitutive relation (stress–strain) under the 1D assumption in the frequency-domain for S waves.\n\n## Propagator Matrix Method\n\nPropagator matrix approaches assign a matrix to every layer in the site, which depends on the mechanical properties of the material and the ratio between the wavelength of the propagating plane wave to the thickness of the layer, and these matrices link stresses and displacements in one border of the layer to the other.\n\nThis method allows obtaining \"transfer functions\" to relate the displacement amplitude at the free surface to either the displacement at a deeper stratum or to the amplitude of the wave that impinges on the shallow layers coming from an idealized half-space.\n\n## Closed-Form Solutions\n\nThe mathematical structure of transfer functions comes defined as a superposition of independent harmonics, whose number, amplitudes and periods are fully characterized in terms of the properties of the layers in closed-form.\n\nFrequency domain methods are simple and concise to be implemented in site response analysis, and in the implementation process, a closed form solution to the wave propagation equation by which shear waves propagate through a layered continuous medium can be presented.\n\n## For Acceleration Transfer Functions\n\nWithin the profile, both upward and downward travelling waves exist due to reflection at each layer boundary, and transfer functions can be used to calculate various response parameters, such as acceleration, velocity, displacement, shear stress, and shear strain, under an input motion parameter such as a bedrock acceleration.\n\nThe most detailed mathematical treatment appears to be in the peer-reviewed literature on analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils, which provides both the general formulation and specific closed-form expressions for various boundary conditions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed3db8e9-bddf-493a-90e5-1f46bbf22517","uuid":"3a60beec-a40a-4dc8-b5e1-defe26c5b2eb","timestamp":"2026-03-29T13:48:14.834Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"1D shear wave propagation uniform soil layer free surface rock base transfer function acceleration closed form equation derivation","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01AMEcTWkndv5mgSWBTF4usm","content":[{"title":"Analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0267726122003773"},{"title":"Theory and Examples — TransferFunctionTool 1.0.0 documentation","url":"https://nheri-simcenter.github.io/TFT-Documentation/theory.html"},{"title":"One dimensional (1D) shear wave propagation through a soil layer | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/One-dimensional-1D-shear-wave-propagation-through-a-soil-layer_fig24_308942249"},{"title":"Analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils","url":"https://idus.us.es/bitstreams/f5bf7b6a-8d1f-466a-99fb-6f80bc91b63c/download"},{"title":"Dispersive SH-surface waves in soil deposits of variable shear modulus - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726105800041"},{"title":"Simulation of the propagation of a cylindrical shear wave","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.04750"},{"title":"17th World Conference on Earthquake Engineering, 17WCEE","url":"https://wcee.nicee.org/wcee/article/17WCEE/4f-0001.pdf"},{"title":"PACIFIC EARTHQUAKE ENGINEERING RESEARCH CENTER Technical Manual for Strata","url":"https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf"},{"title":"Example 2: Multi-Material Site Response Analysis with Transfer Function Comparison — Femora (Fast Efficient Meshing for OpenSees-based Resilience Analysis) 0.5.0 documentation","url":"https://femora.io/introduction/SiteResponseExamples/example2.html"},{"title":"Analysis of Nonlinear Soil-Structure Interaction Effects on ...","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1601.02352"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found comprehensive information about 1D shear wave propagation transfer functions for uniform soil layers with free surfaces and rock bases. Here's what the research literature covers:\n\n## Key Theoretical Framework\n\nThe method considers balance of linear momentum (equilibrium) and the constitutive relation (stress–strain) under the 1D assumption in the frequency-domain for S waves.\n\n## Propagator Matrix Method\n\nPropagator matrix approaches assign a matrix to every layer in the site, which depends on the mechanical properties of the material and the ratio between the wavelength of the propagating plane wave to the thickness of the layer, and these matrices link stresses and displacements in one border of the layer to the other.\n\nThis method allows obtaining \"transfer functions\" to relate the displacement amplitude at the free surface to either the displacement at a deeper stratum or to the amplitude of the wave that impinges on the shallow layers coming from an idealized half-space.\n\n## Closed-Form Solutions\n\nThe mathematical structure of transfer functions comes defined as a superposition of independent harmonics, whose number, amplitudes and periods are fully characterized in terms of the properties of the layers in closed-form.\n\nFrequency domain methods are simple and concise to be implemented in site response analysis, and in the implementation process, a closed form solution to the wave propagation equation by which shear waves propagate through a layered continuous medium can be presented.\n\n## For Acceleration Transfer Functions\n\nWithin the profile, both upward and downward travelling waves exist due to reflection at each layer boundary, and transfer functions can be used to calculate various response parameters, such as acceleration, velocity, displacement, shear stress, and shear strain, under an input motion parameter such as a bedrock acceleration.\n\nThe most detailed mathematical treatment appears to be in the peer-reviewed literature on analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils, which provides both the general formulation and specific closed-form expressions for various boundary conditions."],"durationSeconds":9.082104625000037}}
