{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AMoGkwgbsqrYqtCGy2Ufgd","type":"tool_result","content":"# Mathematical Formulas for Seismic Shear Strains\n\n## Governing Equations\n\nThe foundational relationship uses modal superposition to express seismic shear strain across depth:\n\n**\"γ_yz(t) = Σ γ'_n(ξ) · P_n · D_n(t)\"**\n\nwhere γ'_n represents the modal shear strain shape, P_n is the participation factor, and D_n(t) denotes relative displacement history.\n\n## Shear Modulus Distribution\n\nMaterial inhomogeneity is captured through depth-dependent modulus variation:\n\n**\"G = G_b·ξ^m\"**\n\nwhere ξ = z/H (normalized depth), G_b is base shear modulus, and m ranges from 0.35 to 0.90 depending on material characteristics.\n\n## Strain as Displacement Derivative\n\nShear strain is obtained by differentiating displacement with respect to depth:\n\n**\"γ_yz(ξ;t) = ∂u/∂z\"**\n\nThis fundamental relationship connects kinematic deformation to strain response.\n\n## Steady-State Closed-Form Expression\n\nFor harmonic excitation, the authors derived:\n\n**\"γ_yz(ξ) = [(q+1)/(2-m)] · [J_q+1(a₀√(1-a₀²m/2))] / [a₀J_q(a₀)]\"**\n\nwhere Bessel functions J_p describe the modal response characteristics and a₀ represents dimensionless frequency."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed3db8e9-bddf-493a-90e5-1f46bbf22517","uuid":"18ca7552-8b2b-4342-bdd0-acf769f8c60a","timestamp":"2026-03-29T13:48:51.415Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":299466,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Mathematical Formulas for Seismic Shear Strains\n\n## Governing Equations\n\nThe foundational relationship uses modal superposition to express seismic shear strain across depth:\n\n**\"γ_yz(t) = Σ γ'_n(ξ) · P_n · D_n(t)\"**\n\nwhere γ'_n represents the modal shear strain shape, P_n is the participation factor, and D_n(t) denotes relative displacement history.\n\n## Shear Modulus Distribution\n\nMaterial inhomogeneity is captured through depth-dependent modulus variation:\n\n**\"G = G_b·ξ^m\"**\n\nwhere ξ = z/H (normalized depth), G_b is base shear modulus, and m ranges from 0.35 to 0.90 depending on material characteristics.\n\n## Strain as Displacement Derivative\n\nShear strain is obtained by differentiating displacement with respect to depth:\n\n**\"γ_yz(ξ;t) = ∂u/∂z\"**\n\nThis fundamental relationship connects kinematic deformation to strain response.\n\n## Steady-State Closed-Form Expression\n\nFor harmonic excitation, the authors derived:\n\n**\"γ_yz(ξ) = [(q+1)/(2-m)] · [J_q+1(a₀√(1-a₀²m/2))] / [a₀J_q(a₀)]\"**\n\nwhere Bessel functions J_p describe the modal response characteristics and a₀ represents dimensionless frequency.","durationMs":6342,"url":"https://www.academia.edu/103879197/Seismic_shear_strains_and_seismic_coefficients_in_dams_and_embankments"}}
