{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G5TFP2npGsmo7gzxLgAQQw","type":"tool_result","content":"# Mathematical Formulas from \"1D Harmonic Response of Layered Inhomogeneous Soil\"\n\n## Wave Equation and Shear Modulus\n\nThe governing differential equation for one-dimensional shear wave propagation is:\n\n```\nd/dz[G(z)·du/dz] + ρω²u = 0\n```\n\nwhere G(z) represents the depth-dependent shear modulus, ρ is soil density, ω is cyclic frequency, and u is displacement.\n\n## Power-Law Velocity Model\n\nThe shear wave velocity follows a generalized parabolic relationship:\n\n```\nVs = VH[b + (1-b)(z/H)^(1/n)]\n```\n\nwhere b = (Vo/VH)^(1/n), with Vo and VH representing surface and base velocities, H is layer thickness, and n is the inhomogeneity factor.\n\n## Bessel-Type Solution\n\nAfter coordinate transformation, the displacement field satisfies:\n\n```\nd²u/dx² + (1/x)·du/dx + (kr²zr/q)·x^(2n)·u = 0\n```\n\nThe general solution employs Bessel functions of the first and second kind: \"uðxÞ ¼ xᵐ[C₁Jₙ(lx^(ℓ/2)) + C₂Nₙ(lx^(ℓ/2))]\"\n\n## Natural Frequencies\n\nNormalized fundamental frequency expression:\n\n```\nωm/(ω1n) = (1-n)(1-b)λm\n```\n\nwhere ω₁ₙ = πVH/(2H) represents reference frequency.\n\n## Mode Shapes\n\nNormalized modal shape function:\n\n```\nFm(z) = [b^m + (1-b)(z/H)]^m · [numerator]/[denominator]\n```\n\ninvolving Bessel function ratios evaluated at eigenvalues λₘ.\n\n## Base-to-Surface Transfer Function\n\nFor single inhomogeneous layer:\n\n```\nF(ω) = (2b^(mℓ/2))/[πℓ] · [Jₙ₊₁(λb^(ℓ/2))Nₙ(λ) - Jₙ(λ)Nₙ₊₁(λb^(ℓ/2))]⁻¹\n```\n\nFor two-layer systems, the function incorporates continuity conditions and homogeneous layer response characteristics.\n\n## Key Finding on Surface Strains\n\nThe analysis reveals that \"with zero stiffness at the surface, shear strains may become infinite when the inhomogeneity factor exceeds 0.5, while surface displacements remain finite.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed3db8e9-bddf-493a-90e5-1f46bbf22517","uuid":"d705a6c2-57b8-4ac2-8b8c-10dba32c02da","timestamp":"2026-03-29T13:49:07.410Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":312143,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Mathematical Formulas from \"1D Harmonic Response of Layered Inhomogeneous Soil\"\n\n## Wave Equation and Shear Modulus\n\nThe governing differential equation for one-dimensional shear wave propagation is:\n\n```\nd/dz[G(z)·du/dz] + ρω²u = 0\n```\n\nwhere G(z) represents the depth-dependent shear modulus, ρ is soil density, ω is cyclic frequency, and u is displacement.\n\n## Power-Law Velocity Model\n\nThe shear wave velocity follows a generalized parabolic relationship:\n\n```\nVs = VH[b + (1-b)(z/H)^(1/n)]\n```\n\nwhere b = (Vo/VH)^(1/n), with Vo and VH representing surface and base velocities, H is layer thickness, and n is the inhomogeneity factor.\n\n## Bessel-Type Solution\n\nAfter coordinate transformation, the displacement field satisfies:\n\n```\nd²u/dx² + (1/x)·du/dx + (kr²zr/q)·x^(2n)·u = 0\n```\n\nThe general solution employs Bessel functions of the first and second kind: \"uðxÞ ¼ xᵐ[C₁Jₙ(lx^(ℓ/2)) + C₂Nₙ(lx^(ℓ/2))]\"\n\n## Natural Frequencies\n\nNormalized fundamental frequency expression:\n\n```\nωm/(ω1n) = (1-n)(1-b)λm\n```\n\nwhere ω₁ₙ = πVH/(2H) represents reference frequency.\n\n## Mode Shapes\n\nNormalized modal shape function:\n\n```\nFm(z) = [b^m + (1-b)(z/H)]^m · [numerator]/[denominator]\n```\n\ninvolving Bessel function ratios evaluated at eigenvalues λₘ.\n\n## Base-to-Surface Transfer Function\n\nFor single inhomogeneous layer:\n\n```\nF(ω) = (2b^(mℓ/2))/[πℓ] · [Jₙ₊₁(λb^(ℓ/2))Nₙ(λ) - Jₙ(λ)Nₙ₊₁(λb^(ℓ/2))]⁻¹\n```\n\nFor two-layer systems, the function incorporates continuity conditions and homogeneous layer response characteristics.\n\n## Key Finding on Surface Strains\n\nThe analysis reveals that \"with zero stiffness at the surface, shear strains may become infinite when the inhomogeneity factor exceeds 0.5, while surface displacements remain finite.\"","durationMs":8344,"url":"https://www.academia.edu/16861773/1D_harmonic_response_of_layered_inhomogeneous_soil_Analytical_investigation"}}
